{
  "brief_rationale": "The trajectory shows the doctor actively assessing current foot/whole-body status (RC1), recognizing high-risk diabetic-foot context (RC2), identifying and narrowing information gaps (RC3), giving explicit escalation thresholds (RC4), clarifying medication/wound care safety (RC5), building an executable follow-up plan (RC6), addressing transport/cost barriers (RC7), and closing safely without overreach (RC8). No in-chain error is present. Multiple high-order points are triggered and completed: HO1 (tiered plan around transport/cost/walking pressure), HO2 (repairing broken medication/wound-care responsibility chain), HO3 (bounded closure separating immediate foot tasks from later imaging surveillance), and HO4 (discovering hidden prior amputation/vascular procedure and chest CT findings). All four HOs show meaningful trajectory impact with distinct citations. The closure is safe, executable, traceable, and valid. The trajectory satisfies strict full-credit conditions for Grade 5 (Perfect) with at least two completed HOs, zero in-chain errors, and all dimensions at least good.",
  "closure_assessment": {
